 "kernel": "the field of a homogeneously polarized cuboid in its own frame (centered, sides along the axes): source {dimension (3,) in m, polarization J (3,) in T}, observer (3,) in m -> B (3,) in T and H (3,) in A/m (src/magpylib_material_response/_array_api/cuboid.py, magpylib's _BHJM_magnet_cuboid written against the array API)",
 "comparison": "Per point and field F (B, H): |got - expected| <= rtol |expected| + atol_F (Euclidean norms), atol_F 0 where absent. A non-finite expected component must be matched exactly: NaN by NaN, inf by inf of the same sign. Expected values are exact (the implementation run at 50 digits on mpmath, rounded to double), except at points marked convention, where no exact value is defined and the conventions below say what decides it; there they are numpy's. atol_F appears where 16 times numpy's own error passes the rtol floor, rounded up to a power of ten. A point with needs depends on that fix: a checker against a library without it skips the point and reports it.",
 "rtol": 1e-12,
 "conventions": [
  "On a face (| |x_k| - d_k/2 | < 1e-15 d_k/2 on one axis k, and |x_j| - d_j/2 < 1e-15 d_j/2 on the other two) B jumps by J: the side taken is the one IEEE signed zeros give in magpylib's formulas, which use atan2. Outside the cuboid, in a face's plane, the field is continuous and exact.",
  "On edges and corners, and for a zero dimension or polarization, the field is (0, 0, 0), signed as polarization * 0 (NaN stays NaN).",
  "Inside (|x| - d/2 < 1e-15 d/2 on every axis, faces included) H = (B - J) / mu_0."
 ],
